“We will need,” said Dumbledore very quietly to the bird, “a warning.”
There was a flash of fire and the phoenix had gone.
Dumbledore now swooped down upon one of the fragile silver instruments
whose function Harry had never known, carried it over to his desk, sat down
facing them again, and tapped it gently with the tip of his wand.
The instrument tinkled into life at once with rhythmic clinking noises. Tiny
puffs of pale green smoke issued from the minuscule silver tube at the top.
Dumbledore watched the smoke closely, his brow furrowed, and after a few
